Let in-flight DMA drain before the RP1 PIO teardown disables the state machine.

Rp1PioDeinit disabled the state machine while DMA still held queued words, so the transfer never
completed and the next PIO session, in this process or the next, waited forever in
rp1_pio_sm_xfer_data32_user. Waiting until the TX FIFO stays empty for 5 ms, at most 0.5 s, lets the
transfer finish into the running state machine. Measured on a Pi 5: without it, PIO opens after a
clean close alternate between working and stalling; with it, every open works.

diff --git a/lib/rp1/rp1_pio_backend.cc b/lib/rp1/rp1_pio_backend.cc
--- a/lib/rp1/rp1_pio_backend.cc
+++ b/lib/rp1/rp1_pio_backend.cc
@@ -746,6 +746,12 @@ void Rp1PioDeinit() {
   Rp1PioState &state = s_pio_state;
   if (!state.active) return;

+  // Let in-flight DMA drain into the running state machine: disabling it with words still queued
+  // leaves the transfer unfinished, and the next PIO session waits on it forever.
+  for (int quiet = 0, waited = 0; quiet < 50 && waited < 5000; waited++) {
+    quiet = pio_sm_is_tx_fifo_empty(state.pio, state.sm) ? quiet + 1 : 0;
+    usleep(100);
+  }
   pio_sm_set_enabled(state.pio, state.sm, false);
   pio_sm_clear_fifos(state.pio, state.sm);
   ReleasePinDirection(state.pio, state.sm, state.used_mask);
